{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4df","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07bd4-2401-7280-a034-9d657f7825ef"},"sequence":202,"recorded_at":1780531400000399,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"c2c31914-ab73-4f56-a747-9109399dc0be","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"c2c31914-ab73-4f56-a747-9109399dc0be","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"c2c31914-ab73-4f56-a747-9109399dc0be"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"498f22b0-e1b1-4801-8611-4c8bedc672da"},"task_id":"498f22b0-e1b1-4801-8611-4c8bedc672da","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"498f22b0-e1b1-4801-8611-4c8bedc672da","chunk":"Read text file `/tmp/wbpf1v-verdict-rev1.md`.\n(99 line(s) above)\n100|Kill rate on my sample: 5 of 11. The battery's 12/12 is a true statement about\n101|twelve mutants that were aimed at the arms which already had tests.\n102|\n103|**M1b has a real failure scenario, and I built the witness.** Two events appended\n104|through `AppendEvent`, then the two blob *files* swapped so each digest path\n105|holds the other event's bytes. Both blobs individually verify and both carry\n106|`SelfEventID`, so the only clause that can refuse is the digest comparison.\n107|\n108|- unmutated production: `ListEvents` → `session event chain is corrupt` ✓ (the\n109|  clause works)\n110|- under M1b: `ListEvents(swapped blobs) -> <nil>` — **content-addressed blob\n111|  substitution admitted** — while `go test ./internal/sessrepo/` is **ok**.\n112|\n113|A ~40-line test driving that vector through `ListEvents` reddens M1a, M1b and M8\n114|at once.\n115|\n116|**M2 is the append-only obligation itself.** `doc.go` says \"files are\n117|content-addressed and installed no-replace, so the chain is append-only **by\n118|construction**\"; README says \"installed no-replace\". Flip `O_EXCL` to `O_TRUNC`\n119|and nothing in the repository notices. It is load-bearing: the `sessrepo` mutex\n120|is per-process, and the suite itself models a second process as a second `Open`\n121|over one root — two processes calling `CreateSession` with different records for\n122|one session ID both pass `os.Stat(directory)`, and `O_EXCL` is the only thing\n123|that stops the second from overwriting `record.json`. AC row 4 names\n124|`TestCreateSessionRefusesDuplicateAndRewrite` and `…RefusesSequenceRepeat`, but\n125|those are stopped by the `Stat` and the sequence arm, never by `O_EXCL`.\n126|\n127|This is the DoD bullet \"Every gate ships at least one NARROWING mutant\" failing\n128|for two gates (the load-path corruption gate and the no-replace install gate),\n129|and it is the DoD/G-B bullet \"no production path can rewrite or delete an\n130|existing entry, **proven by a negative test**\" resting on absence-of-a-method\n131|plus an unwitnessed `O_EXCL`.\n132|\n133|## F3 (blocking, G-B) — an interrupted create bricks the session ID and the whole listing\n134|\n135|`CreateSession` performs four durable steps (`MkdirAll` dir → `writeExclusive`\n136|record → `MkdirAll` events → `writeChain`). Both wired crash points sit outside\n137|that span: `BeforeWrite`/`PointPrepareEnter` fires before the first byte and\n138|`AfterCommit`/`PointCommitApply` after the last. `resumeCreateLocked` exists for\n139|exactly the in-between window but handles only one of its two sub-windows\n140|(record written, chain missing) and is driven by a hand-planted directory, never\n141|by a crash point.\n142|\n143|The other sub-window — crash after the directory exists, before `record.json` —\n144|is unrecoverable. Driven through production entries:\n145|\n146|```\n147|CreateSession(retry)  -> session already exists: session 0198f4c8-…-1234567890ab already exists\n148|GetRecord(same id)    -> session event chain is corrupt: read chain index …: no such file or directory\n149|ListSessions()        -> session event chain is corrupt: read chain index …: no such file or directory\n150|```\n151|\n152|`CreateSession` reports a session that was never created as existing, and there\n153|is no entry that can clear it. Worse, `ListSessions` fails closed on the first\n154|corrupt directory — deliberate and documented — so one interrupted create takes\n155|down the listing surface for **every** session in the repository.\n156|\n157|Both DoD crash bullets are satisfied in letter (a fault before the durable write\n158|and a fault after it are both driven). The gap is that a multi-write operation\n159|was wired with a two-point vocabulary that structurally cannot express its own\n160|recovery window, and the resume logic written for that window is half-covered.\n161|\n162|## F4 (non-blocking, G-A) — the allowlist admits any directory named `sessrepo`\n163|\n164|`sessrepoLeaf := sep + \"sessrepo\" + sep` matched with `strings.Contains` is a\n165|segment match — the substring plant is correctly refused (P1 above) — but it\n166|admits **any** path segment named `sessrepo`, not the owning directory. Planting\n167|`internal/provhost/sessrepo/plant.go` with a production Verify call:\n168|\n169|```\n170|attestation scan: 132 production files, 5 parsed, 4 session-leaf call sites, 0 elsewhere\n171|PASS\n172|```\n173|\n174|Four allowlisted sites where the real leaf has three. The comment says \"the one\n175|directory whose production `VerifyObjectIdentity` calls the rewritten bound\n176|admits\". Anchor it to the module-relative path (`internal/sessrepo/`) so the\n177|allowlist means what it says.\n178|\n179|## F5 (non-blocking, G-C) — reuse is right for the five named owners; the \"no new path policy\" claim is not\n180|\n181|Against the brief's G-C list, `sessrepo` is clean: it reuses `environ`\n182|(`DecodeStrictObject`, `CheckUUIDv7`, `CheckUint53Bounds`), `canonicaljson`\n183|(`VerifyObjectIdentity`), `scalar` (`ParseDigest`, `ParseUUIDv7`,\n184|`ParseUUIDv4`), `secconftest` points/outcomes/`Fault`, and `invcore` census\n185|machinery, with no new decoder, no new bound check, and no new AST walker.\n186|`secprim` is genuinely not needed: session IDs pass UUIDv7 grammar before any\n187|join, and `ListSessions` skips symlinked entries via `DirEntry.IsDir()`.\n188|\n189|But the outcome's \"No new decoder, bound check, **path policy**, AST walker, or\n190|journal: atomicity is temp-file + fsync + rename + dir-fsync\" is not accurate.\n191|`internal/localstore` already owns that policy — \"the AX owner-local path layout\n192|and immutable SHA-256 blob sink\" — and this CR re-implements it:\n193|\n194|| sessrepo | localstore |\n195||---|---|\n196|| `store.go syncDirectory` | `paths.go:535 syncDirectory` — same body |\n197|| `writeAtomic` temp+fsync+rename+dir-fsync | `object_store.go` staged write + `syncDirectory` |\n198|| `writeExclusive` `O_EXCL` install | `projection.go:339` `O_EXCL` + `syncDirectory` |\n199|| `installEventBlob` content-addressed idempotent install | `ObjectStore.PutBlob` |\n200|| `Open(dataRoot)/sessions` layout | `ResolvePaths` / `PathClass` |\n201|\n202|The two copies already disagree: sessrepo skips the directory fsync on\n203|`runtime.GOOS == \"windows\"`, localstore does not. `localstore` has no production\n204|consumer yet, so I am not asking you to rewire this leaf through it — but say so\n205|as a stated bound with the reason, instead of claiming no new path policy exists.\n206|\n207|## What I did not re-run\n208|\n209|Full fuzz smoke (`-fuzztime=100x`) — I judged the producer's skip rationale and\n210|agree with it (no fuzz target and no fuzzed package is touched), so I did not\n211|substitute a run for the judgement. Catalog freshness and the six `cigate`\n212|contract selections ran only as part of the unmasked `go test ./...`, not as\n213|separate named invocations.\n214|\n215|## Rework scope\n216|\n217|1. Make the F1 claim true or make it accurate, and pin whichever you choose.\n218|2. Drive the three unwitnessed clauses at chain.go:278/286 and the `O_EXCL`\n219|   no-replace property, then re-run the battery with the load and recovery paths\n220|   in the denominator. Republish the README battery numbers from that run.\n221|3. Decide what an interrupted create before the record write must do, and wire a\n222|   crash point that can reach the window.\n223|4. Anchor the allowlist to `internal/sessrepo/`.\n224|5. Correct the \"no new path policy\" claim, or reuse `localstore`.\n225|\n226|Items 1 and 2 are the ones that block acceptance. 3 is a product decision on\n227|recovery semantics — if the answer is \"an operator repairs it\", that is a stated\n228|bound, not a fix, but it has to be stated."}}}
